00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:00.913
- Hi, so I'm Emily or M for short,

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.890
and I'm currently doing
my PhD or graduate school.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:08.410
I'm currently in graduate
school at Weill Cornell,

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.400
but I actually do my thesis research

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.410
just down the street
over at Sloan Kettering

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:13.820
in Charles Rudin's lab.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:16.120
And there I studied a type of lung cancer

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.830
called small cell lung cancer.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.560
And in particular,

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:20.640
I'm trying to understand

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.290
how to make immunotherapy
work better for patients

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:24.970
with small cell lung cancer.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:27.657
So immunotherapy is basically
a type of cancer therapy

00:03:27.657 --> 00:03:30.370
that engages our body's immune defenses

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:32.510
to attack cancer cells.

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:35.170
So the kind of immunotherapy
that I'm in particular study

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:37.380
is called immune checkpoint blockade.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.190
And the way it works,

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:41.320
is basically there's this
interaction that happens

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:44.030
between tumor cells and immune cells,

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:45.570
involving two proteins,

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.640
PD-L1 which is on the tumor cell

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.750
and PD1 which is on the immune cell.

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:52.020
And normally these two proteins interact

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.850
actually as a function of
preventing auto-immunity,

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:56.160
which is when our immune cells

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.548
accidentally attack our
bodies' normal healthy cells.

00:04:00.548 --> 00:04:02.870
The tumor cells actually
use this mechanism

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:05.750
as a way to hide themselves
from the immune system,

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:07.450
which would otherwise actually notice

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:09.970
that they're really abnormal
compared to our healthy cells

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:11.980
because they have so many mutations.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.250
So basically we figured out as a therapy

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:18.600
that if you block this
interaction using antibodies,

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:21.140
which can either bind to PD-L1 or PD1

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:23.670
and keep them from binding to each other,

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.210
then you can actually
have the immune cells,

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:27.700
specifically T cells,

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.140
notice the cancer cells.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:31.450
So they can no longer hide
from the immune system

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:33.900
and then they can attack
and kill those cancer cells

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.790
and start healing patients' tumors.

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:38.820
So it's a pretty cool technique

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.250
and as I mentioned,

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:43.310
my work is focused on
using this kind of therapy,

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:45.830
and making it more effective in patients

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:47.230
with small cell lung cancer.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.110
