00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:01.133
Alright.

00:06:01.133 --> 00:06:03.420
So the paper that we're
gonna talk about today

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:05.860
is an example of scientific literature,

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:08.370
but what do we mean by
scientific literature?

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:11.810
So literature as we call
it in the research world

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:15.300
is how scientists basically
write about their research

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:17.180
and then communicate it to each other

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:20.780
in a way that's informative,
expedience and trustworthy.

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:21.730
So as you can imagine,

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:24.210
the first step in generating
scientific literature

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:25.920
is actually doing the research.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:27.460
So scientists will be in the labs,

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:28.400
running experiments,

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:29.490
collecting data,

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:30.410
and then once they decide

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:32.460
they have research they'd like to share,

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:33.980
then they'll write about it in a paper

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:37.290
and then this paper will go to two places.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:39.200
It can go to an open source publisher.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:41.300
A really popular one in life sciences

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:42.840
is called the bioRxiv,

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.270
and papers that go open source
are available for anyone

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:46.780
to read immediately,

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:47.760
which is really helpful

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.840
when the information that
the authors are sharing,

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:53.560
can be used by other
researchers really quickly.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:55.670
Like COVID-19 research

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:58.490
it's especially useful to
have open source journals.

00:06:58.490 --> 00:06:59.800
But a caveat to this

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:03.720
is that the papers that go
to open source publishers

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:05.380
are not peer reviewed.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:08.010
Conversely, another
place that papers can go

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:09.900
is to peer reviewed journals.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:11.760
So you've probably heard of some of these

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:13.290
like Nature, Science,

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:14.590
Cell,

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:15.510
PLOS, PNAS,

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:17.383
there are loads and loads of them.

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:20.840
But here the papers actually
do get peer reviewed

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:22.790
and then once they're
published by the paper,

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:24.100
then they can be read by people

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:25.920
in the scientific and medical community

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:28.010
who will then apply these findings

00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:29.800
towards their own research.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:31.660
So I wanna take a little
bit more time though

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:33.490
to kind of explore the differences

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:35.270
between open source publishing

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:37.750
and peer reviewed journals.

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:39.310
So to kind of talk about peer review

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:41.060
and explain what that actually means.

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:41.893
Basically,

00:07:41.893 --> 00:07:45.282
when authors submit a paper to a journal,

00:07:45.282 --> 00:07:47.645
the editor of that journal
will then forward that paper

00:07:47.645 --> 00:07:50.910
to other experts in the field
that the paper comes from.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:52.200
And those experts will,

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:53.950
who are also scientists,

00:07:53.950 --> 00:07:57.120
will look at the methods
and the data in the paper,

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:00.320
and basically evaluate whether
the experiments were rigorous

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:02.330
or whether more experiments need to happen

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:03.320
in order for the authors

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:07.390
to actually make the claim
that they make in the paper.

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:11.100
So this process can involve
the authors going back,

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:12.401
doing more experiments,

00:08:12.401 --> 00:08:14.200
resubmitting to the journal,

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:15.910
maybe getting rejected by the journal,

00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:17.100
and then submitting to another one

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:19.480
and starting peer review at that journal.

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:21.700
So although peer reviewed
papers are regarded

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:23.830
with more confidence than open source

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:25.860
because they have this
kind of vetting process

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:27.490
that's built in,

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:29.260
it also takes a lot more time

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:30.980
for papers that undergo peer review

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:33.480
to actually be put out
where people can read them.

00:08:34.420 --> 00:08:37.280
Another main difference
between open source publishers

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:38.540
and peer reviewed journals

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:40.310
is the degree of access

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:43.770
that people have to have these papers.

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:48.260
So some journals actually
require that scientists pay them

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:49.440
to publish their papers,

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:53.480
which can then limit where
and whether labs can publish.

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:54.313
And additionally,

00:08:54.313 --> 00:08:56.175
some journals also
require that you pay a fee

00:08:56.175 --> 00:09:00.740
or have a subscription to
read the papers in them,

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:02.170
which can then as you imagine,

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:04.950
also limit who gets to read those papers.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:06.110
A lot of journals nowadays

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:08.700
are moving more towards
an open access model

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:11.630
where you can actually
read their stuff for free.

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:13.768
But overall that whole
situation is avoided

00:09:13.768 --> 00:09:15.723
with open source publishers.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:19.070
And one more kind of limitation to access

00:09:19.070 --> 00:09:21.910
that plagues scientific
literature in general,

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:24.200
no matter where it goes is jargon.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:28.330
So scientists like usually
using a lot of jargon

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:30.330
which are basically technical terms

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:32.210
that are kind of difficult to digest

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:33.940
if you're not a specialist

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:36.880
or not in the fields
that the terms come from,

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:38.050
which can make the literature,

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:39.159
you know, difficult to access for people

00:09:39.159 --> 00:09:41.530
who are not in the fields.

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:44.020
But it's also why we
do data for the people.

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:45.826
So there are ways to work around it.

00:09:45.826 --> 00:09:48.460
(chuckles)

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:50.120
There are also I should say,

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:52.070
various types of scientific literature.

00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:54.040
So the paper that I was just describing

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:56.240
and the paper that we're
gonna look at today,

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:58.433
are both examples of original research.

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:00.470
So I mentioned these,

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:01.327
present the research,

00:10:01.327 --> 00:10:02.920
and the authors hypotheses,

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:04.373
their methods and their data.

00:10:04.373 --> 00:10:06.420
But there are also short
reports and letters

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:09.040
which kind of tell
shorter research stories,

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:10.170
review articles

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:13.120
which kind of summarize
the salient findings

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:15.550
in a single topic or fields,

00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:16.383
case studies,

00:10:16.383 --> 00:10:18.080
which you see a lot in medical journals.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:19.900
And those will cover specific instances

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:22.140
of interesting phenomena like a patient

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:25.420
who has unusual symptoms
for a certain disease.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:27.080
And there are also methods papers

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:29.340
where authors can showcase a new method

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:31.140
or technique that they've developed.

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:33.983
- Alright.

00:10:33.983 --> 00:10:35.220
So moving into the paper

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:37.850
that we are going to
be talking about today.

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:39.007
This paper is called

00:10:39.007 --> 00:10:42.047
"A SARS-CoV-2 protein interaction map

00:10:42.047 --> 00:10:44.160
"reveals targets for drug repurposing"

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:45.680
which is a lot of words.

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:47.660
To break it down a little bit,

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:49.240
in this paper they're going to start

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:51.500
by looking for protein interactions

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:53.560
for the virus SARS-CoV-2,

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:56.830
which we all know is
the novel Corona virus,

00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:59.994
which causes COVID-19 disease.

00:10:59.994 --> 00:11:02.340
And we'll talk more about
what protein interaction

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:04.793
means exactly.
(phone rings)

00:11:04.793 --> 00:11:05.710
And then after that,

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:07.580
they're going to look for drugs

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:09.890
that might be able to be repurposed

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:13.150
or used in a different
way to treat SARS-CoV-2.

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:15.817
And we'll talk a lot more about this.

00:11:15.817 --> 00:11:18.430
And this is a super, super exciting paper,

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:20.000
but it's also still very much

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:22.430
the only beginning of research

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:24.810
and there's a ways to go
before drugs like this

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:28.193
or drugs from this paper could
be used to treat patients.

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:30.250
Another cool thing

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:32.530
and one of the reasons I
wanted to talk about this paper

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:34.750
is because of how fast,

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:35.800
can you go back for one second?

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:37.190
- Oh sorry, (laughs)
- It's fine,

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:38.830
you're right.
(laughs)

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:40.630
It's how fast the research has progressed.

00:11:40.630 --> 00:11:41.500
So you can see,

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:42.520
or maybe you can't see,

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:43.610
but this is actually,

00:11:43.610 --> 00:11:46.460
this is a peer review journal article now.

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:47.640
But when we started talking

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:50.720
about making this presentation
like a week and a half ago,

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:52.650
it was still a preprint.

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:54.090
And one of the reasons why we think

00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:56.210
that this has progressed so quickly

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:59.470
is because of how many authors
