its findings have been
discussed in the New York Times

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:54.780
as well as in the conversation.

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:55.830
So now we're gonna turn things over

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:58.303
and start talking about
the body of the paper.

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:01.163
- Great.

00:13:01.163 --> 00:13:02.650
So before we jump in,

00:13:02.650 --> 00:13:04.150
we'd actually like to
take a little bit of time

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:06.530
to sort of explore some key terms

00:13:06.530 --> 00:13:08.610
that have important roles in the paper,

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:10.390
but that you might be unfamiliar with.

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:13.740
So those terms are
protein-protein interactions,

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:15.380
viral tropism,

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:18.210
phases of drug development
sort of conceptually

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:20.093
and mass spectrometry.

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:24.570
So protein-protein interactions.

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:26.820
So if you think of our cells as factories,

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:28.550
the proteins are the workers.

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:31.150
They're the ones carrying
out all the various functions

00:13:31.150 --> 00:13:32.318
that our cells do,

00:13:32.318 --> 00:13:34.830
to basically keep our bodies working

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:37.493
and healthy and doing
what they need to do.

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:41.760
And a big part of how proteins
do their particular jobs

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:44.520
is by physically
interacting with each other.

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:47.790
So this works at sort of
like 3-D surfaces of proteins

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:48.760
that are complimentary.

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:52.210
So if you imagine puzzle
pieces sticking together,

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:54.110
that's exactly how this works.

00:13:54.110 --> 00:13:56.378
And basically once these
complimentary services

00:13:56.378 --> 00:13:57.670
find each other,

00:13:57.670 --> 00:13:59.770
they then get held
together by chemical bonds

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:01.750
that form in between them.

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:05.470
So in cells proteins physically interact

00:14:05.470 --> 00:14:07.610
to enable certain kinds of jobs.

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:09.340
For example,

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:10.790
some jobs can only take place

00:14:10.790 --> 00:14:12.530
if the proteins are together in a complex

00:14:12.530 --> 00:14:14.713
that are enabled by these interactions.

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:18.631
Sometimes these interactions
can allow one protein

00:14:18.631 --> 00:14:20.810
to make a chemical change to another one

00:14:20.810 --> 00:14:23.563
that then affects the activity
of that other protein.

00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:27.650
Interactions can also allow
proteins to move around the cell

00:14:27.650 --> 00:14:28.483
to different places,

00:14:28.483 --> 00:14:31.120
to do specialized jobs in those places

00:14:32.350 --> 00:14:34.940
and where did it go?

00:14:34.940 --> 00:14:37.040
And protein interactions can assist

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:38.621
or prevent other protein interactions

00:14:38.621 --> 00:14:41.040
that might otherwise be possible.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:44.700
So a very important thing
to know too is that,

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:46.700
lots of drugs work

00:14:46.700 --> 00:14:48.950
by blocking disease-related

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:50.870
protein-protein interactions.

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:51.800
And in our paper,

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:52.746
the authors are not only looking

00:14:52.746 --> 00:14:54.687
for protein-protein interactions,

00:14:54.687 --> 00:14:57.073
but also drugs that can disrupt them.

00:14:58.242 --> 00:15:00.550
Some other term that we
wanted to take a look at

00:15:00.550 --> 00:15:02.250
was viral tropism.

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:05.130
So viral tropism is the
ability of the virus

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:07.130
to infect a specific host.

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:10.390
And this can happen at
various levels of biology.

00:15:10.390 --> 00:15:11.223
For example,

00:15:11.223 --> 00:15:12.400
at the cellular level,

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:15.020
we know that HIV can infect macrophages,

00:15:15.020 --> 00:15:16.630
which is a kind of immune cell,

00:15:16.630 --> 00:15:18.170
but it doesn't infect neurons,

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:19.770
which are cells in our brains.

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:22.640
The flu virus infects our lung tissue,

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:24.010
but not our brain tissue

00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:26.400
and even at the whole organism level.

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:29.480
Myxoma is an example of a
virus that infects rabbits

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:31.360
but doesn't infect us humans.

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:34.220
And basically what
determines whether a virus

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:36.180
can infect a specific host

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:39.380
is the presence of certain
things called host factors.

00:15:39.380 --> 00:15:42.350
So these are cellular
resources that a virus hijacks

00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:45.870
in order to infect and
replicate within its host.

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:47.520
So for SARS-CoV-2,

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:50.070
which we know can affect bats,

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:51.230
monkeys, humans,

00:15:51.230 --> 00:15:53.253
and maybe some house pets.

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:56.650
We know that a host
factor that it relies upon

00:15:56.650 --> 00:15:58.410
is the ACE2 receptor,

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:00.980
which is a protein that
is expressed in our cells

00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:04.140
that SARS-CoV-2 actually
uses to enter our cells

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:05.360
during infection.

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:06.750
So the authors of our paper

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:08.550
are looking for more host factors,

00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:10.690
essentially like ACE2,

00:16:10.690 --> 00:16:13.200
that SARS-CoV-2 viruses need

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:17.263
by looking for viral human
protein interactions.

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:20.253
Alright.

00:16:20.253 --> 00:16:23.390
So the authors of our paper
report that they find drugs

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:25.490
that come from preclinical studies,

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:26.640
clinical trials

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:28.230
and are FDA approved.

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:29.230
So we wanted to take a beat

00:16:29.230 --> 00:16:31.040
and kind of break down what each of these

00:16:31.040 --> 00:16:34.050
sort of phases of drug
development actually means.

00:16:34.050 --> 00:16:35.850
So whenever a lab,

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:36.910
say at a drug

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:38.900
a pharma company,

00:16:38.900 --> 00:16:40.704
finds a drug that they
think could be effective

00:16:40.704 --> 00:16:42.410
in treating some disease,

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:43.560
the first step for that drug

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:45.620
is to go into preclinical studies.

00:16:45.620 --> 00:16:47.090
So at this step,

00:16:47.090 --> 00:16:49.500
the drugs are assessed for safety,

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:50.333
in animals

00:16:50.333 --> 00:16:51.493
oftentimes mice.

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:54.730
And basically during these studies,

00:16:54.730 --> 00:16:56.010
scientists will take measurements

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:57.730
like where the drug goes in the body,

00:16:57.730 --> 00:16:59.750
sort of what organs it hangs out in,

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:01.580
how long it stays there,

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:04.070
whether the drug gets broken
down into smaller molecules

00:17:04.070 --> 00:17:05.930
that might have different effects.

00:17:05.930 --> 00:17:08.690
And once all of this data is collected,

00:17:08.690 --> 00:17:10.170
the FDA then reviews it

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:13.270
and decides whether the drug
can go into clinical trials.

00:17:13.270 --> 00:17:15.430
So clinical trials are what
you hear about in the news

00:17:15.430 --> 00:17:17.780
that are like phase I, II, and III.

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:20.790
All that applies to this
step in the process.

00:17:20.790 --> 00:17:22.090
And these trials take place

00:17:22.090 --> 00:17:23.870
in patients and healthy volunteers

00:17:23.870 --> 00:17:27.110
who receive either the drug or a placebo,

00:17:27.110 --> 00:17:28.350
and their purpose is to make sure

00:17:28.350 --> 00:17:30.780
that the drug is effective and safe.

00:17:30.780 --> 00:17:34.100
And then once the FDA reviews
a whole other bunch of data

00:17:34.100 --> 00:17:35.420
from the clinical trials,

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:37.700
they decide whether to
approve or reject the drug.

00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:38.560
And if it's approved,

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:39.700
then you can buy it.

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:41.300
It goes in the market,

00:17:41.300 --> 00:17:43.580
and it can be prescribed to patients.

00:17:43.580 --> 00:17:46.143
So we have a little poll for you guys.

00:17:47.590 --> 00:17:48.550
On average,

00:17:48.550 --> 00:17:51.450
how long do you think it
takes for a drug to progress

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:54.010
from preclinical studies to FDA approval?

00:17:54.010 --> 00:17:57.528
So one end of that flow chart
all the way to the other.

00:17:57.528 --> 00:18:01.695
(waits for poll results silently)

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:20.933
Alright.

00:18:20.933 --> 00:18:24.000
I think we're starting to level out.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:28.133
I'm gonna close the poll in
like maybe 10 seconds or so.

00:18:29.632 --> 00:18:32.868
Okay.

00:18:32.868 --> 00:18:33.701
Five,

00:18:33.701 --> 00:18:34.534
four,

00:18:34.534 --> 00:18:35.620
three,

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:36.520
two,

00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:37.770
one.

00:18:37.770 --> 00:18:38.603
Alright.

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:41.610
So actually,

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:42.820
oh, this is interesting.

00:18:42.820 --> 00:18:43.653
Okay.

00:18:43.653 --> 00:18:45.870
So three to five years.

00:18:45.870 --> 00:18:47.280
I like the optimism.

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:49.351
It's actually 10 to 12 years.

00:18:49.351 --> 00:18:51.290
(chuckles)

00:18:51.290 --> 00:18:54.540
So this actually kind of
makes our point pretty well

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:58.130
and that that's not an
insignificant amount of time.

00:18:58.130 --> 00:19:01.330
But that's also why this
idea of repurposing drugs.

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:02.370
Oh, sorry.

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:04.200
Okay, maybe now you can see it.

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:05.370
Hold on.

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:06.203
Okay.

00:19:06.203 --> 00:19:07.036
I guess we got it.

00:19:07.036 --> 00:19:07.869
Okay.

00:19:07.869 --> 00:19:11.600
But that's why the idea of
repurposing drugs is so appealing

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:13.117
because if, you know, if you
have like a brand new drug

00:19:13.117 --> 00:19:15.420
that's still to undergo
preclinical studies,

00:19:15.420 --> 00:19:16.850
all the clinical trials and everything,

00:19:16.850 --> 00:19:18.840
that can take 10 to 12 years.

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:19.673
But if say,

00:19:19.673 --> 00:19:22.090
if you have a drug that's
already FDA approved,

00:19:22.090 --> 00:19:24.190
even though it would have to be reapproved

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:25.323
to be used in a different disease

00:19:25.323 --> 00:19:27.480
than the one that it was
originally approved for,

00:19:27.480 --> 00:19:30.530
because we already have some
data from preclinical studies,

00:19:30.530 --> 00:19:32.700
we have an idea of how
safe it is in people

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:34.280
and what doses are appropriate,

00:19:34.280 --> 00:19:37.700
that process from starting
to getting it approved

00:19:37.700 --> 00:19:40.580
is a lot faster than it would
be if the drug was brand new.

00:19:40.580 --> 00:19:42.150
So that's why we're
trying to look for drugs

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:43.500
that we can repurpose for COVID,

00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:45.370
so we can get them into the clinic

00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:47.020
and to our patients more quickly.

00:19:48.790 --> 00:19:49.623
- Alright.

00:19:49.623 --> 00:19:52.060
So this last vocab word
that we're gonna go over

00:19:52.060 --> 00:19:53.710
or vocab concept

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:58.310
is a method that the authors use.

00:19:58.310 --> 00:19:59.330
So in this paper,

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:01.870
the authors are trying to
identify the human proteins

00:20:01.870 --> 00:20:05.660
that interact with viral proteins
as we've mentioned before.

00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:08.310
But the scientists have to figure out

00:20:08.310 --> 00:20:12.620
how do they go from having
this unknown protein,

00:20:12.620 --> 00:20:14.600
how do they identify it?

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:17.870
And so the tool that
they use to identify it,

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:18.703
click please,

00:20:19.880 --> 00:20:22.040
is a tool known as mass spectrometry

00:20:22.040 --> 00:20:23.270
or a mass spec for short

00:20:23.270 --> 00:20:26.373
because spectrometry is like
kind of a mouthful to say.

00:20:27.220 --> 00:20:29.900
And mass spec is a tool
that scientists can use

00:20:29.900 --> 00:20:33.290
to identify proteins as
well as other molecules.

00:20:33.290 --> 00:20:37.420
And in this paper it
is used for the authors

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:38.770
to be able to figure out

00:20:38.770 --> 00:20:41.900
what human proteins interact
with viral proteins.

00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:42.810
Next slide.

00:20:42.810 --> 00:20:45.340
So going into a little
bit more about what,

00:20:45.340 --> 00:20:47.540
how mass spectrometry works.

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:51.080
Spectrometry is a big word as I mentioned,

00:20:51.080 --> 00:20:52.380
hard to say.

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:55.210
But it really describes a
method of studying a spectrum

00:20:55.210 --> 00:20:56.460
like in this case mass.

00:20:56.460 --> 00:20:59.480
Other types of spectrometry
can look at things like color

00:20:59.480 --> 00:21:00.800
or fluorescence.

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:03.780
And a mass spectrometry works

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:06.370
by measuring how much
mass there is an object

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:07.500
depending on it's charge.

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:09.840
So it basically is used to calculate

00:21:09.840 --> 00:21:13.660
the mass to charge
ratio for each molecule.

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:15.355
So for-

00:21:15.355 --> 00:21:16.210
- Right.
(whisphering)

00:21:16.210 --> 00:21:17.200
- Sorry.
(chuckles)

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:20.760
So we have some videos
kind of to illustrate this

00:21:20.760 --> 00:21:22.850
'cause I know I'm not
gonna like act it out.

00:21:22.850 --> 00:21:24.460
That would be weird.

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:26.502
So to...

00:21:26.502 --> 00:21:27.335
(chuckles)

00:21:27.335 --> 00:21:29.430
The first video is to show
the first step of mass spec,

00:21:29.430 --> 00:21:31.260
which is ionization.

00:21:31.260 --> 00:21:32.660
So as you can see here,

00:21:32.660 --> 00:21:35.950
a mass spectrometer starts
by breaking up a molecule

00:21:35.950 --> 00:21:37.140
in this case of protein,

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:40.373
into smaller bits called ions.

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:44.730
And so now all of these smaller circles

00:21:44.730 --> 00:21:47.600
or spheres have broken up.

00:21:47.600 --> 00:21:49.800
They have to be sorted.

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:53.640
And so these ions or in
the video little circles

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:56.600
go through an electromagnet
which separates them

00:21:56.600 --> 00:21:58.740
on the basis of their mass and charge.

00:21:58.740 --> 00:22:01.450
So you can see that in this video,

00:22:01.450 --> 00:22:03.860
the little blue balls are traveling faster

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:05.970
and the red ones are traveling slower

00:22:06.870 --> 00:22:08.220
because they're bigger.

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:10.150
And then finally,

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:12.860
all of these sorted ions
need to be detected.

00:22:12.860 --> 00:22:15.180
And so in this step,

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:17.950
the mass to charge ratio
for each ion is graphed

00:22:17.950 --> 00:22:20.440
so that we can see how many ions

00:22:20.440 --> 00:22:24.190
of each mass to charge ratio
there are in the sample.

00:22:24.190 --> 00:22:26.550
And so the results from
a mass spectrometer

00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:28.630
kind of look like this piecemeal graph

00:22:28.630 --> 00:22:31.760
where you see how big
each individual ion is,

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:34.830
and then you can put that
information together,

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:38.720
sort of like a puzzle
to identify the protein

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:41.993
that you put in at the beginning.

00:22:43.150 --> 00:22:43.983
And so

00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:47.210
now that we kind of have a basis

00:22:47.210 --> 00:22:49.910
of the concepts in this paper,

00:22:49.910 --> 00:22:53.000
the two questions that the
authors are trying to ask are

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:53.833
first,

00:22:53.833 --> 00:22:54.967
"What human proteins interact

00:22:54.967 --> 00:22:57.610
"with the SARS-CoV-2 viral proteins?"

00:22:57.610 --> 00:22:58.457
And second,

00:22:58.457 --> 00:23:00.917
"Are there drugs against
these human proteins

00:23:00.917 --> 00:23:03.697
"that can be repurposed
to treat COVID-19?"

00:23:04.900 --> 00:23:07.040
And how are they going to do this?

00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:09.600
They use a type of scientific reasoning

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:12.930
that is known as experimental evaluation.

00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:14.060
There's lots of different ways

00:23:14.060 --> 00:23:17.080
that scientific knowledge can be created,

00:23:17.080 --> 00:23:19.430
lots of different approaches for that.

00:23:19.430 --> 00:23:22.180
These authors use a series
of controlled experiments

00:23:22.180 --> 00:23:25.800
to understand what viral
and human proteins interact

00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:27.950
and what drugs might
be potentially helpful

00:23:27.950 --> 00:23:29.913
in disrupting those interactions.

00:23:31.110 --> 00:23:34.120
And so to actually
perform these experiments,

00:23:34.120 --> 00:23:35.330
the authors began,

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:36.910
oh sorry, next slide.

00:23:36.910 --> 00:23:38.160
Thank you.
(chuckles)

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:39.490
To perform these experiments,

00:23:39.490 --> 00:23:41.783
the authors began by
identifying human proteins

00:23:41.783 --> 00:23:43.950
that interact with viral proteins.

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:45.974
So they looked into virus' genome

00:23:45.974 --> 00:23:47.770
or genetic material.

00:23:47.770 --> 00:23:50.940
And some of these viral
proteins are already known.

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:52.940
And then the others were predicted

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:55.450
based off of what we
know about Corona viruses

00:23:55.450 --> 00:23:57.803
and DNA translation in general.

00:23:59.210 --> 00:24:00.043
And so,

00:24:02.370 --> 00:24:03.307
okay.

00:24:03.307 --> 00:24:06.550
And so obviously no one really wants

00:24:06.550 --> 00:24:10.480
to get those virus proteins
put into their bodies,

00:24:10.480 --> 00:24:14.080
to see what proteins the virus
interacts with in humans.

00:24:14.080 --> 00:24:18.260
And so a way that scientists
can still study human cells

00:24:18.260 --> 00:24:20.220
is to use something called a cell line.

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:22.150
And so cell lines are cells

00:24:22.150 --> 00:24:24.290
that are originally
taken from an organism,

00:24:24.290 --> 00:24:25.500
in this case humans,

00:24:25.500 --> 00:24:27.760
that are treated with a
bunch of different things

00:24:27.760 --> 00:24:30.010
so they can grow indefinitely in the lab,

00:24:30.010 --> 00:24:34.390
generally in some kind of
plastic plate or something.

00:24:34.390 --> 00:24:37.810
And so most cell lines
have been kept in labs

00:24:37.810 --> 00:24:38.960
for a really long time.

00:24:38.960 --> 00:24:43.280
So there are some caveats
because the cells aren't exactly,

00:24:43.280 --> 00:24:44.950
they don't have all the same properties

00:24:44.950 --> 00:24:46.650
as a cell in your body would have,

00:24:46.650 --> 00:24:48.930
but they are kind of the best we have

00:24:48.930 --> 00:24:50.890
because we're not gonna go around

00:24:50.890 --> 00:24:53.073
putting viral proteins into people.

00:24:54.630 --> 00:24:55.810
And so in this case,

00:24:55.810 --> 00:24:58.520
the authors just use a
human kidney cell line

00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:01.860
and they express a single
viral protein at a time.

00:25:01.860 --> 00:25:03.050
And so again,

00:25:03.050 --> 00:25:04.940
this isn't exactly like what would happen

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:06.180
during a viral infection.

00:25:06.180 --> 00:25:10.330
Normally viruses don't
only infect your cells

00:25:10.330 --> 00:25:12.130
with one protein at a time,

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:13.350
but this helps the authors

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:16.490
to identify the specific interactions

00:25:16.490 --> 00:25:19.793
between a single viral protein
and the human proteins.

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:24.410
And so once all these human
cells were expressing,

00:25:24.410 --> 00:25:26.220
or they made the viral protein,

00:25:26.220 --> 00:25:28.170
they burst open the human cells

00:25:28.170 --> 00:25:30.330
and they took out all of the proteins.

00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:31.420
And they did this in a way

00:25:31.420 --> 00:25:34.260
so that all of the proteins
could still be interacting

00:25:34.260 --> 00:25:35.093
with each other.

00:25:35.093 --> 00:25:35.990
So they maintained all those

00:25:35.990 --> 00:25:38.850
human protein-viral protein interactions.

00:25:38.850 --> 00:25:42.210
And then from this protein
soup that they made,

00:25:42.210 --> 00:25:44.530
they pulled out the viral proteins

00:25:44.530 --> 00:25:46.650
and everything that was attached to them.

00:25:46.650 --> 00:25:48.480
And so from there,

00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:51.810
they have the viral protein
connected with the human protein

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:53.450
and they can use mass spectrometry,

00:25:53.450 --> 00:25:55.090
which I talked about earlier,

00:25:55.090 --> 00:25:57.950
to identify the human proteins.

00:25:57.950 --> 00:26:00.750
And finally they put all
of this data together

00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:03.160
into these really pretty graphs

00:26:03.160 --> 00:26:05.610
that Emily's going to talk
about in much more detail.

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:08.583
But these maps show the
protein-protein interruptions.

00:26:11.670 --> 00:26:12.503
- Alright.

00:26:12.503 --> 00:26:15.830
So in total of the 26 SARS-CoV-2 proteins,

00:26:15.830 --> 00:26:19.140
the authors express in these human cells,

00:26:19.140 --> 00:26:23.810
they find 332 human proteins
that interact with them.

00:26:23.810 --> 00:26:26.480
And then of those 332 human proteins,

00:26:26.480 --> 00:26:28.210
66 were druggable,

00:26:28.210 --> 00:26:29.370
which means that we have,

00:26:29.370 --> 00:26:32.190
we know of drugs that can bind to them.

00:26:32.190 --> 00:26:34.120
So then they looked at all
those drugs that emerged

00:26:34.120 --> 00:26:36.110
and you can see they come
from those various phases

00:26:36.110 --> 00:26:38.600
of drug development that we
talked about a little while ago,

00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:40.890
and they picked 47 of those drugs

00:26:40.890 --> 00:26:43.610
to move into Viral Infectivity Assays.

00:26:43.610 --> 00:26:46.370
So basically these are
experiments that look at the drugs

00:26:46.370 --> 00:26:48.170
and see if they can,

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:50.530
if they reduce the ability of SARS-CoV-2

00:26:50.530 --> 00:26:52.730
to infect cells in any way,

00:26:52.730 --> 00:26:54.690
which we'd want to see them do,

00:26:54.690 --> 00:26:57.940
if we thought they could be
effective in COVID-19 patients.

00:26:57.940 --> 00:27:00.650
So to kind of break down
a little bit further,

00:27:00.650 --> 00:27:02.210
what that data look like.

00:27:02.210 --> 00:27:04.330
So these are two of those interaction maps

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:06.210
that Lindsey mentioned.

00:27:06.210 --> 00:27:08.190
As you can see there's a
lot of information here

00:27:08.190 --> 00:27:10.550
and there are actually
26 of these in the paper,

00:27:10.550 --> 00:27:11.900
one for each viral protein.

00:27:11.900 --> 00:27:12.733
So overall,

00:27:12.733 --> 00:27:13.700
this paper generated,

00:27:13.700 --> 00:27:15.790
you know, these authors I should say,

00:27:15.790 --> 00:27:18.370
generated a ton of data.

00:27:18.370 --> 00:27:19.252
But just to kind of break down

00:27:19.252 --> 00:27:20.940
what we're actually looking at,

00:27:20.940 --> 00:27:24.060
so we chose sort of two viral proteins,

00:27:24.060 --> 00:27:27.600
or interaction maps as examples

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:28.710
of the data.

00:27:28.710 --> 00:27:31.620
One of the viral proteins is ORF9c,

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:34.120
which is a protein the
authors predicted to exist

00:27:34.120 --> 00:27:36.680
based on looking at the SARS-CoV-2 genome.

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:38.680
And the other one is NSP6,

00:27:38.680 --> 00:27:41.090
which we know is involved in replicating

00:27:41.090 --> 00:27:42.900
the SARS-CoV-2 genome.

00:27:42.900 --> 00:27:46.210
So each of these viral proteins
are here as the red diamonds

00:27:46.210 --> 00:27:48.080
and then kind of at the periphery,

00:27:48.080 --> 00:27:50.440
in gray we can see the human proteins

00:27:50.440 --> 00:27:53.500
that don't have known
drugs that bind to them,

00:27:53.500 --> 00:27:54.520
at least yet.

00:27:54.520 --> 00:27:55.990
And in yellow,

00:27:55.990 --> 00:27:57.890
are the proteins that do have drugs

00:27:57.890 --> 00:27:59.390
that we know can bind to them.

00:28:00.240 --> 00:28:02.610
Also pretty cool are these spokes

00:28:02.610 --> 00:28:05.480
that connect the human
proteins to the viral protein.

00:28:05.480 --> 00:28:07.860
So these based on their
shading and thickness,

00:28:07.860 --> 00:28:10.960
actually give information about
the statistical confidence

00:28:10.960 --> 00:28:12.220
that the authors calculated

00:28:12.220 --> 00:28:14.600
between each of these interactions.

00:28:14.600 --> 00:28:15.490
So if you look down here

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:17.580
this MIST Score for example,

00:28:17.580 --> 00:28:22.340
is a statistical calculation
between each interaction.

00:28:22.340 --> 00:28:23.620
And then,

00:28:23.620 --> 00:28:25.420
I think the coolest part of these at least

00:28:25.420 --> 00:28:28.480
are these sort of blue
clouds that you see,

00:28:28.480 --> 00:28:31.300
kind of like in these clusters
of different human proteins.

00:28:31.300 --> 00:28:34.240
So these indicate that the
proteins in the cluster

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:36.950
all share the same biological process

00:28:36.950 --> 00:28:38.720
and those are written here in bold.

00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:39.860
So for example,

00:28:39.860 --> 00:28:42.080
these three proteins
that are in this cloud

00:28:42.080 --> 00:28:44.190
are all involved in ion transport,

00:28:44.190 --> 00:28:46.330
which is basically just
moving chemical ions

00:28:46.330 --> 00:28:50.220
back and forth between
membranes inside of our cells.

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:51.690
And the sort of cool thing

00:28:51.690 --> 00:28:55.420
about identifying these
shared biological processes

00:28:55.420 --> 00:28:56.700
is that they give us an idea

00:28:56.700 --> 00:29:00.210
of not only the proteins that
SARS-CoV-2 interacts with,

00:29:00.210 --> 00:29:03.700
but also possibly the general
functions in our cells

00:29:03.700 --> 00:29:05.660
that SARS-CoV-2 is hijacking

00:29:05.660 --> 00:29:08.000
when it's infecting and
replicating our cells

00:29:08.950 --> 00:29:09.783
or in our cells.

00:29:11.640 --> 00:29:14.180
So here are the same two interaction maps,

00:29:14.180 --> 00:29:15.710
that we looked at before.

00:29:15.710 --> 00:29:18.780
Only now the druggable
proteins are highlighted

00:29:18.780 --> 00:29:20.760
and the drugs that match them are listed

00:29:20.760 --> 00:29:22.290
in these little text boxes.

00:29:22.290 --> 00:29:24.340
So you can see the FDA approved drugs

00:29:24.340 --> 00:29:26.720
that some of them might
sound a bit familiar.

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:27.553
For example,

00:29:27.553 --> 00:29:30.740
Metformin is a drug for type-2 diabetes.

00:29:30.740 --> 00:29:34.530
Haloperidol or haldol is
an antipsychotic drug.

00:29:34.530 --> 00:29:37.190
Chloroquine an anti-malarial,

00:29:37.190 --> 00:29:39.620
is also in a relative
of hydroxychloroquine

00:29:41.310 --> 00:29:45.140
that's usually used for patients
with autoimmune disorders

00:29:45.140 --> 00:29:47.720
like lupus or rheumatoid arthritis.

00:29:47.720 --> 00:29:49.680
And they've also both been
in the news quite a bit

00:29:49.680 --> 00:29:52.143
over the past few months for COVID-19.

00:29:52.980 --> 00:29:54.180
So one cool thing to note.

00:29:54.180 --> 00:29:55.950
Well, one thing to note I should say

00:29:55.950 --> 00:29:57.750
with these FDA approved drugs is that

00:29:57.750 --> 00:30:00.060
that's great as we heard
a little while ago.

00:30:00.060 --> 00:30:01.020
If they are effective,

00:30:01.020 --> 00:30:03.350
they can be approved
for COVID-19 much faster

00:30:03.350 --> 00:30:06.420
than a drug that was just made brand new.

00:30:06.420 --> 00:30:07.900
But this also means,

00:30:07.900 --> 00:30:09.850
or I guess this can also mean that

00:30:09.850 --> 00:30:11.220
because these drugs were designed

00:30:11.220 --> 00:30:13.190
for different purposes originally

00:30:13.190 --> 00:30:14.430
and for a different disease,

00:30:14.430 --> 00:30:16.440
that they could have effects in our bodies

00:30:16.440 --> 00:30:18.230
apart from treating COVID-19,

00:30:18.230 --> 00:30:20.880
even if we take them for COVID-19.

00:30:20.880 --> 00:30:22.730
But that's the sort of thing
that would be figured out

00:30:22.730 --> 00:30:24.053
in clinical trials.

00:30:27.170 --> 00:30:28.003
- Alright.

00:30:28.003 --> 00:30:31.640
So now that these authors
have found all of these drugs

00:30:31.640 --> 00:30:35.150
that could potentially be
useful in treating the virus

00:30:35.150 --> 00:30:38.640
by disrupting viral protein-human
protein interactions,

00:30:38.640 --> 00:30:41.190
they wanted to test and see if these drugs

00:30:41.190 --> 00:30:45.563
actually impacted the ability
of the virus to infect cells.

00:30:46.750 --> 00:30:49.900
Again, the caveat is cells aren't people,

00:30:49.900 --> 00:30:51.670
they're not organisms.

00:30:51.670 --> 00:30:54.330
But this is a common first
step that scientists use

00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:56.810
to see if it's worth
spending the resources

00:30:56.810 --> 00:31:00.150
to test these drugs in more preclinical

00:31:00.150 --> 00:31:02.030
and clinical applications.

00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:04.070
And so what they did is

00:31:04.070 --> 00:31:07.930
they took cells this time
from a monkey cell line,

00:31:07.930 --> 00:31:11.230
and they began treating them
with the drug of interest.

00:31:11.230 --> 00:31:13.180
And so

00:31:13.180 --> 00:31:14.290
after two hours,

00:31:14.290 --> 00:31:15.123
the drugs,

00:31:15.123 --> 00:31:17.020
they wanted to ask the question,

00:31:17.020 --> 00:31:18.370
if the drug prevents

00:31:18.370 --> 00:31:22.710
or decreases the ability of
the virus to infect the cell.

00:31:22.710 --> 00:31:23.858
So after two hours,

00:31:23.858 --> 00:31:26.020
they infected the drug treated cells

00:31:26.020 --> 00:31:28.840
and control cells that
don't have drug treatment

00:31:28.840 --> 00:31:29.963
with the virus.

00:31:31.050 --> 00:31:34.070
And they kind of let the virus
do its thing for two days

00:31:34.070 --> 00:31:37.020
and the ultimate readout
was looking to see

00:31:37.020 --> 00:31:39.780
if viral infection was reduced.

00:31:39.780 --> 00:31:42.640
And they did this.

00:31:42.640 --> 00:31:44.560
So the expectation is

00:31:44.560 --> 00:31:48.850
if the drug in fact does
prevent viral infection,

00:31:48.850 --> 00:31:51.350
you would see a reduction
of viral infection

00:31:51.350 --> 00:31:53.650
or maybe even no viral infection at all.

00:31:53.650 --> 00:31:55.800
Whereas if the drug didn't
actually do anything,

00:31:55.800 --> 00:31:58.020
which is totally possible,

00:31:58.020 --> 00:32:00.600
then viral infection would
still occur normally.

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:02.770
And they measured the level of virus

00:32:02.770 --> 00:32:04.930
by looking at the viral proteins

00:32:04.930 --> 00:32:07.233
and the levels of viral RNA.

00:32:11.610 --> 00:32:15.920
So the authors took those 69
drugs that they identified.

00:32:15.920 --> 00:32:17.320
Some of them being FDA approved,

00:32:17.320 --> 00:32:18.340
some in clinical trials,

00:32:18.340 --> 00:32:19.550
some preclinical,

00:32:19.550 --> 00:32:22.403
and they tested 47 of them

00:32:22.403 --> 00:32:24.300
in this infectivity assay

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:27.970
to see if the drug prevented
or decreased viral infection.

00:32:27.970 --> 00:32:29.270
And so this is an example

00:32:29.270 --> 00:32:32.040
of what one of those assays looks like.

00:32:32.040 --> 00:32:34.810
This is a drug called PB28.

00:32:34.810 --> 00:32:37.590
And just to walk you
through this graph a little.

00:32:37.590 --> 00:32:39.660
I know there's a lot going on.

00:32:39.660 --> 00:32:42.987
The black line corresponds
with the number of living cells

00:32:42.987 --> 00:32:44.040
and this is important

00:32:44.040 --> 00:32:46.189
because we don't want the
drug to kill the cells.

00:32:46.189 --> 00:32:49.700
We just want it to
prevent viral infection.

00:32:49.700 --> 00:32:54.500
And the red line is the
level of viral proteins.

00:32:54.500 --> 00:32:56.890
And then on the y-axis we have percent.

00:32:56.890 --> 00:32:58.680
So the way you would read this graph is

00:32:58.680 --> 00:32:59.970
the black line corresponds

00:32:59.970 --> 00:33:02.260
to the percentage of cells that are alive

00:33:02.260 --> 00:33:03.760
and the red line corresponds

00:33:03.760 --> 00:33:08.290
with the percentage of
viral proteins in the cell.

00:33:08.290 --> 00:33:10.850
And then on the x-axis we
have the amount of drug.

00:33:10.850 --> 00:33:12.620
And so this is also important.

00:33:12.620 --> 00:33:16.310
You might imagine that giving
a lot of drug to the cells,

00:33:16.310 --> 00:33:19.970
might lead to the virus
infecting the cells less,

00:33:19.970 --> 00:33:22.960
but it also could be more
toxic to the cells and kill,

00:33:22.960 --> 00:33:24.860
be more likely to kill them.

00:33:24.860 --> 00:33:26.160
And so in this example,

00:33:26.160 --> 00:33:28.670
the PB28 drugs seems
to reduce infectivity,

00:33:28.670 --> 00:33:30.320
which is awesome.

00:33:30.320 --> 00:33:34.160
You can see that by the
decrease in the red line

00:33:34.160 --> 00:33:36.380
over the amount of drug.

00:33:36.380 --> 00:33:37.970
And so,

00:33:37.970 --> 00:33:39.210
but this is just one example.

00:33:39.210 --> 00:33:42.160
Not all of those 47 drugs that they tested

00:33:42.160 --> 00:33:45.190
did actually reduce infectivity.

00:33:45.190 --> 00:33:46.710
And what it would look like

00:33:46.710 --> 00:33:48.490
if the drug didn't reduce infectivity

00:33:48.490 --> 00:33:50.930
is you would have like a flat red line

00:33:50.930 --> 00:33:53.083
just to show that there was no effect.

00:33:54.100 --> 00:33:58.770
And so of the drugs that did
seem to reduce infectivity,

00:33:58.770 --> 00:34:02.010
there were two major
classes or two like groups.

00:34:02.010 --> 00:34:03.530
The first being drugs that bind

00:34:03.530 --> 00:34:05.730
the sigma-1 sigma-2 receptor,

00:34:05.730 --> 00:34:09.380
and the second being drugs that
prevent making new protein.

00:34:09.380 --> 00:34:11.684
So PB28 is actually an example

00:34:11.684 --> 00:34:13.510
of that first class of drugs

00:34:13.510 --> 00:34:15.943
that bind to the sigma-1 sigma-2 receptor.

00:34:17.140 --> 00:34:19.650
And then you would
imagine it kind of like,

00:34:19.650 --> 00:34:20.483
I don't know,

00:34:20.483 --> 00:34:21.760
hand-wavy kind of makes sense

00:34:21.760 --> 00:34:24.440
that drugs that prevent
making new proteins

00:34:24.440 --> 00:34:27.293
might also prevent viral infection.

00:34:30.520 --> 00:34:31.353
- Alright.

00:34:31.353 --> 00:34:32.186
So now that we've looked at

00:34:32.186 --> 00:34:35.010
at least some representative
data from the paper,

00:34:35.010 --> 00:34:36.510
let's revisit those
questions that we mentioned

00:34:36.510 --> 00:34:37.470
at the beginning.

00:34:37.470 --> 00:34:39.916
So question one that the
authors set out to ask

00:34:39.916 --> 00:34:43.447
in their study is "What
human proteins interact

00:34:43.447 --> 00:34:46.100
"with SARS-CoV-2 viral proteins?"

00:34:46.100 --> 00:34:47.447
And then question two,

00:34:47.447 --> 00:34:49.487
"Are there drugs against
these human proteins

00:34:49.487 --> 00:34:52.420
"that could be repurposed for COVID-19?"

00:34:52.420 --> 00:34:54.710
So one thing that I think is generally

00:34:54.710 --> 00:34:55.586
kind of a good practice to take

00:34:55.586 --> 00:34:57.308
whenever you're looking at a study,

00:34:57.308 --> 00:34:59.804
is not only see what
the authors found out,

00:34:59.804 --> 00:35:02.030
but also what they didn't find out.

00:35:02.030 --> 00:35:04.410
You know, every paper that's out there,

00:35:04.410 --> 00:35:05.480
will present a lot of data,

00:35:05.480 --> 00:35:07.418
but also leave some questions unanswered.

00:35:07.418 --> 00:35:09.992
And by kind of consciously
being aware of those,

00:35:09.992 --> 00:35:12.956
you cannot only see exactly how far,

00:35:12.956 --> 00:35:15.370
the study pushed our knowledge forward,

00:35:15.370 --> 00:35:16.410
but also keep an eye out

00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:18.270
for the things that it didn't answer.

00:35:18.270 --> 00:35:21.301
And then for the research
that's working to answer it,

00:35:21.301 --> 00:35:22.860
and follow up.

00:35:22.860 --> 00:35:24.687
So for question one,

00:35:24.687 --> 00:35:28.340
we can say the authors did
identify human proteins

00:35:28.340 --> 00:35:30.030
332 of them,

00:35:30.030 --> 00:35:32.720
that SARS-CoV-2 proteins interact with.

00:35:32.720 --> 00:35:34.330
And they also showed evidence

00:35:34.330 --> 00:35:35.640
that some of these interactions

00:35:35.640 --> 00:35:39.840
are important to SARS-CoV-2
viral infectivity.

00:35:39.840 --> 00:35:41.300
And actually the authors work

00:35:41.300 --> 00:35:43.840
in identifying these human proteins

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:46.433
or SARS-CoV-2 host factors,

00:35:47.540 --> 00:35:48.373
actually,

00:35:48.373 --> 00:35:50.210
well, not only points us towards drugs

00:35:50.210 --> 00:35:52.320
that might be useful in treating COVID-19

00:35:52.320 --> 00:35:55.842
because they bind the human proteins,

00:35:55.842 --> 00:35:57.267
but they also advance our understanding

00:35:57.267 --> 00:35:58.962
about the kinds of cell functions

00:35:58.962 --> 00:36:02.537
that SARS-CoV-2 hijacks
when it's in our cells.

00:36:02.537 --> 00:36:03.907
And this understanding could also help us

00:36:03.907 --> 00:36:06.115
progress towards therapy itself

00:36:06.115 --> 00:36:08.920
because although we know that

00:36:08.920 --> 00:36:10.498
well, we have reason to think that

00:36:10.498 --> 00:36:12.332
drugs that bind these specific proteins

00:36:12.332 --> 00:36:14.540
they found might be disruptive,

00:36:14.540 --> 00:36:15.373
we can also look for drugs

00:36:15.373 --> 00:36:17.470
that disrupt these general functions

00:36:17.470 --> 00:36:20.199
that might've not been
captured in this paper.

00:36:20.199 --> 00:36:21.840
And we can also test those

00:36:21.840 --> 00:36:25.030
and see if they're
therapeutically viable as well.

00:36:25.030 --> 00:36:26.740
So for question two,

00:36:26.740 --> 00:36:29.070
the authors also identified drugs

00:36:29.070 --> 00:36:30.500
that might be able to disrupt

00:36:30.500 --> 00:36:33.130
the human viral protein interactions.

00:36:33.130 --> 00:36:36.500
And at least in their assays
that Lindsey just showed us

00:36:36.500 --> 00:36:39.930
some of these drugs
showed antiviral activity.

00:36:39.930 --> 00:36:42.010
But very importantly,

00:36:42.010 --> 00:36:44.420
the authors did not find a treatment

00:36:44.420 --> 00:36:47.890
that we can start giving to
COVID-19 patients right away.

00:36:47.890 --> 00:36:51.230
And that's because the
conditions in their experiments

00:36:51.230 --> 00:36:53.270
compared to the conditions
in our human bodies

00:36:53.270 --> 00:36:56.160
are so different that
we can't possibly effect

00:36:56.160 --> 00:36:58.170
or we can't possibly predict rather

00:36:58.170 --> 00:37:00.930
all of the effects that
these drugs would have

00:37:00.930 --> 00:37:02.730
in a COVID-19 patient.

00:37:02.730 --> 00:37:04.620
So as Lindsey mentioned,

00:37:04.620 --> 00:37:06.040
one way in which this is the case

00:37:06.040 --> 00:37:08.920
is that all these experiments
happened in cell lines.

00:37:08.920 --> 00:37:11.950
And cell lines are
immortalized as we call it,

00:37:11.950 --> 00:37:13.700
which means they can grow in a lab,

00:37:13.700 --> 00:37:14.920
in some plastic,

00:37:14.920 --> 00:37:16.935
in a solution with like sugar,

00:37:16.935 --> 00:37:20.060
and just do that indefinitely
and be really happy.

00:37:20.060 --> 00:37:23.100
And as you can imagine for
cells to be able to do that,

00:37:23.100 --> 00:37:24.870
they have to take on a certain like

00:37:24.870 --> 00:37:26.700
some certain behaviors
that are much different

00:37:26.700 --> 00:37:30.060
than the cells in our bodies actually do.

00:37:30.060 --> 00:37:33.670
So that already makes the
system quite a bit different.

00:37:33.670 --> 00:37:35.230
in terms of the experimental system

00:37:35.230 --> 00:37:37.370
from what the human body would be like.

00:37:37.370 --> 00:37:40.610
Another caveat is that these
cells also don't account

00:37:40.610 --> 00:37:44.520
for certain variables that
would be very important

00:37:44.520 --> 00:37:46.640
to how patients respond to this therapy.

00:37:46.640 --> 00:37:48.500
So when you think about it,

00:37:48.500 --> 00:37:50.550
like patients can have all sorts

00:37:50.550 --> 00:37:52.840
of different genetic
differences between them

00:37:52.840 --> 00:37:54.460
just from one person to another.

00:37:54.460 --> 00:37:55.888
They can have other medical conditions,

00:37:55.888 --> 00:37:58.550
they can even be on other drugs

00:37:58.550 --> 00:37:59.818
to treat those medical conditions.

00:37:59.818 --> 00:38:03.350
And all of those would
not be sort of factored in

00:38:03.350 --> 00:38:06.320
in the experiments that are
done in these cell lines

00:38:06.320 --> 00:38:07.170
and would also have an effect

00:38:07.170 --> 00:38:10.608
on how these patients respond
to COVID-19 therapies.

00:38:10.608 --> 00:38:13.410
Now this isn't to say that like,

00:38:13.410 --> 00:38:15.960
you know, the results in
this study are not useful

00:38:15.960 --> 00:38:17.410
or they're unreliable.

00:38:17.410 --> 00:38:18.530
To the contrary,

00:38:18.530 --> 00:38:20.820
I mean, just based on
those two interactives,

00:38:20.820 --> 00:38:21.653
as I said,

00:38:21.653 --> 00:38:22.486
there were 26 of those.

00:38:22.486 --> 00:38:24.960
So there was a lot of data in this paper,

00:38:24.960 --> 00:38:27.070
it came out super fast as like a testament

00:38:27.070 --> 00:38:29.390
to this power of this collaboration.

00:38:29.390 --> 00:38:31.030
And there's a lot here
that'll be very useful

00:38:31.030 --> 00:38:33.980
to other researchers in
the field moving forward.

00:38:33.980 --> 00:38:35.440
And it's also not to say too,

00:38:35.440 --> 00:38:36.570
with the drugs they found

00:38:36.570 --> 00:38:40.900
won't necessarily be
useful to treat COVID-19.

00:38:40.900 --> 00:38:42.530
It's just that we can't say that yet

00:38:42.530 --> 00:38:45.250
without actually testing
them in clinical trials.

00:38:45.250 --> 00:38:46.730
So on that note,

00:38:46.730 --> 00:38:48.110
let's take a look,

00:38:48.110 --> 00:38:49.910
sort of hypothetically at one of the drugs

00:38:49.910 --> 00:38:51.220
that came from this paper

00:38:51.220 --> 00:38:53.450
to see what its path might look like

00:38:53.450 --> 00:38:55.200
through the clinical trial process.

00:38:56.750 --> 00:38:59.150
So there's a lot going on here,

00:38:59.150 --> 00:39:01.860
but we'll break it down
and work through it.

00:39:01.860 --> 00:39:04.563
So as I mentioned at
the top of the webinar,

00:39:05.570 --> 00:39:08.980
when a drug kind of like after
it's developed in the lab

00:39:08.980 --> 00:39:11.070
and is like ready to go
into clinical trials,

00:39:11.070 --> 00:39:12.160
or at least hopefully,

00:39:12.160 --> 00:39:15.800
and it undergoes this
preclinical testing in animals.

00:39:15.800 --> 00:39:18.220
And then once the company
runs these preclinical tests,

00:39:18.220 --> 00:39:20.950
they gather all that data
and submit it to the FDA

00:39:20.950 --> 00:39:25.020
in what's called an Investigational
New Drug application.

00:39:25.020 --> 00:39:27.430
So this application has all that data

00:39:27.430 --> 00:39:30.290
and it also has description
of the company's plans

00:39:30.290 --> 00:39:32.570
for actually running
those clinical trials.

00:39:32.570 --> 00:39:35.290
So if the FDA approves this application

00:39:35.290 --> 00:39:38.300
then the drug gains
Investigational New Drug status,

00:39:38.300 --> 00:39:39.420
which basically just means

00:39:39.420 --> 00:39:41.620
that it can be shipped across state lines

00:39:41.620 --> 00:39:44.040
to be used in clinical trials.

00:39:44.040 --> 00:39:45.540
So then the drug goes

00:39:45.540 --> 00:39:46.999
into these phase I,

00:39:46.999 --> 00:39:48.411
II and III

00:39:48.411 --> 00:39:51.310
at a minimum clinical trials.

00:39:51.310 --> 00:39:53.630
And as I mentioned before,

00:39:53.630 --> 00:39:54.980
clinical trials take place

00:39:54.980 --> 00:39:57.238
in healthy volunteers and
patients with the disease

00:39:57.238 --> 00:39:59.461
depending upon the phase.

00:39:59.461 --> 00:40:01.620
They'll be treated with
a drug or a placebo

00:40:01.620 --> 00:40:03.260
and then their response is to be monitored

00:40:03.260 --> 00:40:05.230
over a set period of time.

00:40:05.230 --> 00:40:07.850
So clinical trials are designed

00:40:07.850 --> 00:40:11.300
to figure out two very
specific things about a drug.

00:40:11.300 --> 00:40:12.133
Firstly,

00:40:12.133 --> 00:40:13.090
it's efficacy,

00:40:13.090 --> 00:40:15.490
which is basically how good the drug is

00:40:15.490 --> 00:40:19.110
at treating the disease
that it's supposed to treat,

00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:20.550
in terms of alleviating symptoms

00:40:20.550 --> 00:40:23.280
or healing the patient depending
on what the drug is for.

00:40:23.280 --> 00:40:25.900
And it also measures the
toxicity of the drug,

00:40:25.900 --> 00:40:28.490
which means the side effects the drug has

00:40:28.490 --> 00:40:31.280
and how severe those side effects are.

00:40:31.280 --> 00:40:33.960
So in phase I clinical trials,

00:40:33.960 --> 00:40:35.970
which typically lasts
about several months,

00:40:35.970 --> 00:40:38.508
the drug is tested in healthy volunteers

00:40:38.508 --> 00:40:40.410
and here they're looking for

00:40:40.410 --> 00:40:42.030
they're looking at the safety of the drug

00:40:42.030 --> 00:40:43.566
and they're also deciding dosing ranges

00:40:43.566 --> 00:40:46.440
that will be appropriate
to test in patients

00:40:46.440 --> 00:40:47.830
in the next phase.

00:40:47.830 --> 00:40:50.039
So once the drug passes phase I,

00:40:50.039 --> 00:40:51.830
then it goes into phase II,

00:40:51.830 --> 00:40:53.620
and now it's given to patients

00:40:53.620 --> 00:40:56.730
with the disease the drug is
actually designed to treat.

00:40:56.730 --> 00:40:59.120
So here we have quite
a few more participants

00:41:00.636 --> 00:41:03.400
in this phase than in the previous one.

00:41:03.400 --> 00:41:05.730
And it also lasts a bit longer,

00:41:05.730 --> 00:41:08.550
going from several
months like up to a year.

00:41:08.550 --> 00:41:10.884
And then following phase II,

00:41:10.884 --> 00:41:13.540
then the drug moves into
phase III clinical trials,

00:41:13.540 --> 00:41:15.223
which is still in patients
that have the disease

00:41:15.223 --> 00:41:16.880
that the drug is meant to treat,

00:41:16.880 --> 00:41:18.690
but now there are way more of them

00:41:18.690 --> 00:41:20.630
on the order of hundreds to thousands.

00:41:20.630 --> 00:41:23.170
And this phase also
takes quite a bit longer,

00:41:23.170 --> 00:41:25.550
usually in the realm of one to four years.

00:41:25.550 --> 00:41:28.160
So here as in phase II,

00:41:28.160 --> 00:41:30.128
they're still looking
at the drugs efficacy

00:41:30.128 --> 00:41:31.770
and its toxicity,

00:41:31.770 --> 00:41:33.780
and kind of narrowing down doses

00:41:33.780 --> 00:41:35.781
that would be appropriate
to use in the clinic,

00:41:35.781 --> 00:41:37.500
once the drug is approved.

00:41:37.500 --> 00:41:39.070
But in phase III in particular,

00:41:39.070 --> 00:41:41.199
they're also looking for rare side effects

00:41:41.199 --> 00:41:43.310
that you'd be less likely to find

00:41:43.310 --> 00:41:44.992
when you're testing the
drug in fewer people

00:41:44.992 --> 00:41:46.720
as you are in phase II.

00:41:46.720 --> 00:41:49.580
So when you see drug
commercials at the very end,

00:41:49.580 --> 00:41:51.030
the narrator kind of like whispers

00:41:51.030 --> 00:41:53.450
extra side effects at you like a secret.

00:41:53.450 --> 00:41:56.820
A lot of those show up in
phase III clinical trials.

00:41:56.820 --> 00:42:00.020
So after all the clinical
trial data is gathered

00:42:00.020 --> 00:42:01.420
and the trials are done,

00:42:01.420 --> 00:42:03.620
then the company packages that up

00:42:03.620 --> 00:42:06.330
into what's called a New Drug Application.

00:42:06.330 --> 00:42:09.450
They're usually at least
100,000 pages long.

00:42:09.450 --> 00:42:11.310
So it's a lot of data

00:42:11.310 --> 00:42:13.060
and the FDA reviews that

00:42:13.060 --> 00:42:13.970
and then at that point,

00:42:13.970 --> 00:42:16.350
either approves or rejects the drug,

00:42:16.350 --> 00:42:17.903
at which point it can be marketed

00:42:17.903 --> 00:42:19.646
and it can be used to treat patients.

00:42:19.646 --> 00:42:21.196
So as I mentioned before,

00:42:21.196 --> 00:42:24.420
FDA approved drugs that are
approved for one disease

00:42:24.420 --> 00:42:27.000
must be reapproved before they can be used

00:42:27.000 --> 00:42:28.940
to treat a different disease.

00:42:28.940 --> 00:42:31.740
So now to look at one of
our hypothetical drugs,

00:42:31.740 --> 00:42:32.630
PB28,

00:42:32.630 --> 00:42:35.557
is the one that Lindsey showed
us some infectivity data for

00:42:35.557 --> 00:42:37.610
that looked really good.

00:42:37.610 --> 00:42:40.060
But because it's not an approved drug,

00:42:40.060 --> 00:42:41.220
it's actually quite far

00:42:41.220 --> 00:42:42.610
at the very beginning of the pipeline.

00:42:42.610 --> 00:42:45.250
It will be entering here
in the preclinical phase.

00:42:45.250 --> 00:42:46.700
And as we learned at the beginning,

00:42:46.700 --> 00:42:48.530
that means it could be like 10 to 12 years

00:42:48.530 --> 00:42:50.980
between this drug actually
entering the pipeline

00:42:50.980 --> 00:42:53.430
and coming out at the other
end and being FDA approved

00:42:53.430 --> 00:42:55.723
if it makes it through
the pipeline at all.

00:42:57.440 --> 00:42:59.530
By contrast remdesivir,

00:42:59.530 --> 00:43:01.390
which is a drug for COVID-19,

00:43:01.390 --> 00:43:03.770
possible that's being tested for COVID-19

00:43:03.770 --> 00:43:06.550
that's been in the news
quite a bit lately,

00:43:06.550 --> 00:43:08.018
was developed as a general antiviral

00:43:08.018 --> 00:43:10.146
and it was actually in
phase II clinical trials

00:43:10.146 --> 00:43:11.245
back in 2015,

00:43:11.245 --> 00:43:13.050
during the Ebola outbreak,

00:43:13.050 --> 00:43:16.358
and entered phase III clinical
trials this past February,

00:43:16.358 --> 00:43:19.270
in a trial that included
some COVID-19 patients

00:43:19.270 --> 00:43:22.173
and it's still in that
phase of trials right now.

00:43:23.120 --> 00:43:25.213
And actually there's kind
of a special sort of caveat

00:43:25.213 --> 00:43:26.440
with remdesivir.

00:43:26.440 --> 00:43:28.630
So as of last week,

00:43:28.630 --> 00:43:31.890
because of some preliminary
results that suggest that

00:43:31.890 --> 00:43:33.991
it might be shortening
the duration of disease

00:43:33.991 --> 00:43:35.387
in certain patients

00:43:35.387 --> 00:43:38.090
and the fact that it's just
looking kind of promising,

00:43:38.090 --> 00:43:42.570
the FDA granted what's called
Emergency Use Authorization

00:43:42.570 --> 00:43:43.720
for remdesivir,

00:43:43.720 --> 00:43:47.490
which means that even though
it's not FDA approved yet,

00:43:47.490 --> 00:43:49.360
doctors can prescribe it to patients

00:43:49.360 --> 00:43:51.190
that have severe symptoms.

00:43:51.190 --> 00:43:52.122
So as I mentioned,

00:43:52.122 --> 00:43:54.780
like this rule about approving drugs

00:43:54.780 --> 00:43:56.640
for a new disease still holds true,

00:43:56.640 --> 00:43:58.640
but given the promise of this drug

00:43:58.640 --> 00:44:01.556
and the special circumstances
that we're in with COVID-19,

00:44:01.556 --> 00:44:03.650
there are some exceptions that can be made

00:44:03.650 --> 00:44:05.532
so that way these drugs
that look promising

00:44:05.532 --> 00:44:08.773
can get to patients they
can help even sooner.

00:44:10.480 --> 00:44:11.313
Alright.

00:44:11.313 --> 00:44:13.670
And one last thing that
I want to share here.

00:44:13.670 --> 00:44:15.248
So resources that I
found were pretty helpful

00:44:15.248 --> 00:44:17.260
and kind of just looking into,

00:44:17.260 --> 00:44:18.716
you know, a bunch of
COVID-19 clinical trials

00:44:18.716 --> 00:44:19.920
that are going on

00:44:19.920 --> 00:44:22.270
and sort of preparing for this data

00:44:22.270 --> 00:44:25.100
for the people presentation
I've listed here.

00:44:25.100 --> 00:44:29.450
So statnews.com has a
really nice COVID-19,

00:44:29.450 --> 00:44:30.840
clinical trial tracker.

00:44:30.840 --> 00:44:32.460
It's very reader-friendly,

00:44:32.460 --> 00:44:33.330
really up to date

00:44:33.330 --> 00:44:35.990
and has like nice descriptions
of what the drugs actually do

00:44:35.990 --> 00:44:37.830
and how they actually work.

00:44:37.830 --> 00:44:39.833
The FDA of course has great information

00:44:39.833 --> 00:44:42.214
about how these clinical
trials are happening.

00:44:42.214 --> 00:44:45.729
They also issue frequent press
releases with new findings,

00:44:45.729 --> 00:44:49.470
like that Emergency Use
Authorization that I talked about.

00:44:49.470 --> 00:44:50.410
And lastly,

00:44:50.410 --> 00:44:53.740
if you want to see all of
the COVID-19 trials going on

00:44:53.740 --> 00:44:55.050
all over the world,

00:44:55.050 --> 00:44:56.730
those are available at ClinicalTrials.gov

00:44:57.719 --> 00:45:01.740
and they actually have
them from the WHO database.

00:45:01.740 --> 00:45:02.940
That's the coolest list.

00:45:04.870 --> 00:45:05.703
- Alright.

00:45:05.703 --> 00:45:08.268
So we just wanted to end this
presentation by highlighting

00:45:08.268 --> 00:45:13.020
how this was a totally
massive amount of data

00:45:13.020 --> 00:45:15.961
and the reason why the authors were able

00:45:15.961 --> 00:45:17.500
to generate all these data

00:45:17.500 --> 00:45:21.710
is because of the fact
that there were 125 authors

00:45:21.710 --> 00:45:24.703
in over 20 labs in eight institutions.

00:45:25.900 --> 00:45:28.610
And we also just thought
that it was super nice

00:45:28.610 --> 00:45:29.840
that in addition to the fact that

00:45:29.840 --> 00:45:32.287
there's already this
massive collaboration,

00:45:32.287 --> 00:45:34.301
the authors have also
done a really good job

00:45:34.301 --> 00:45:36.950
of making all parts of their research

00:45:36.950 --> 00:45:39.280
as accessible and transparent as possible,

00:45:39.280 --> 00:45:41.240
including writing in the paper

00:45:41.240 --> 00:45:44.300
what their next step plans
are for testing these drugs.

00:45:44.300 --> 00:45:45.616
And they're also really willing

00:45:45.616 --> 00:45:47.657
to distribute materials to other labs

00:45:47.657 --> 00:45:51.360
so that other labs can
participate in help testing.

00:45:51.360 --> 00:45:53.413
So we wanted to highlight this by,

00:45:54.430 --> 00:45:55.496
one of the quotes from the paper

00:45:55.496 --> 00:45:59.250
that just like made us
all feel a little happy

00:45:59.250 --> 00:46:02.650
and kind of optimistic in
these very uncertain times.

00:46:02.650 --> 00:46:04.307
And this quote says,

00:46:04.307 --> 00:46:06.427
"Given the urgent and acute global need

00:46:06.427 --> 00:46:07.957
"for SARS-CoV-2 research,

00:46:07.957 --> 00:46:09.667
"we will make these resources available

00:46:09.667 --> 00:46:12.407
"to the scientific
community upon completion,

00:46:12.407 --> 00:46:14.560
"as we have done with this study."

00:46:14.560 --> 00:46:16.970
And so we just thought
that was really cool.

00:46:16.970 --> 00:46:18.662
And it's nice to know that scientists

00:46:18.662 --> 00:46:21.020
they're all collaborating
and working together

00:46:21.020 --> 00:46:22.353
to try to figure this out.

